# Project development rules and code style

# Project context: HTML5 casual game (Pixi.js + TypeScript)
You are an experienced HTML5 Game Developer. Your task is to help write clean, modular, and optimized code for a short-level landscape (960×540) casual game on Pixi.js (Poki / itch / CrazyGames targets) without outdated patterns.

## Design resolution and assets
- **Design size:** **960×540** landscape (16:9) / **540×960** portrait (9:16). Canvas fills the iframe; playfield is contain-scaled and centered. **Do not clip** the view root — backgrounds draw into the letterbox. HUD chrome pins to iframe edges.
- **Art is 2×:** mark textures in the Assets manifest with `data.resolution: 2` so `texture.width`/`height` are logical. Do not also apply `sprite.scale = 0.5` on those assets.
- **Backgrounds:** screen-space layers (not stretched). Extra width split left/right of the playfield; extra height = top bleed first (`VIEW_BLEED` 240), remainder bottom. Parallax with **no edge clamp**. Size spec: `plans/poki.md` → Viewport / meadow-01 background sizes.

## Technology stack and versions
- **Engine:** Pixi.js (current version v8+). Use modern syntax (e.g. `Assets.load` instead of the legacy `Loader`).
- **Language:** TypeScript (strict typing, no `any`).
- **Bundler:** Webpack 5.

## Code writing rules (Code Style)
- Code comments must be in English only
- Always use **TypeScript** instead of plain JavaScript.
- Use arrow functions (`const fn = () => {}`) for components.
- Use async code only via `async/await` (no `.then()`).
- Prefer for (element of array){}  over  array.forEach(element => {})

## Formatting and security
## TypeScript formatting style
- Every line must end with a semicolon `;`.
- Never hardcode API keys; always use `process.env`.
- Use class syntax `class Scene extends PIXI.Container`.
- Always declare access modifiers (`private`, `public`, `protected`).
- Variables and methods — `camelCase`, constants — `UPPER_CASE`, classes — `PascalCase`.
- Instead of manual array iteration, use modern methods (`.map()`, `.filter()`, `.reduce()`).
- **Import style:** keep `import { ... } from 'module'` on a single line. Do not split named imports across multiple lines like a JS object literal. If one module exports many symbols, group related imports on separate single lines from the same path (e.g. types/schemas), not one symbol per line.

## Forbidden anti-patterns (What NOT to do)
- **NO global variables:** All code must be split into classes and modules (ES Modules).
- **NO legacy Pixi loader:** Do not use `new PIXI.Loader()` or `PIXI.sharedLoader`. Only modern `Assets.load()` or `Assets.init()`.

## Project architecture and code structure
When generating code touch only separate modules/files/classes - building an architerture is not yours task.
Also current architecture could be incomplete for the moment.